body:
The new Temporal implementation uses the host time zone for `UTCDate` and `UTCDateMini`, rather than UTC. This can change the ISO week-year.

On main `717ce0a807ea4c6b540d015b5c408723175b2838`, with Node 26.10.0 and `TZ=America/Los_Angeles`:

```ts
import { UTCDate } from "@date-fns/utc";
import { endOfISOWeekYear } from "./pkgs/core/src/endOfISOWeekYear/index.ts";
import { tpyEndOfISOWeekYear } from "./pkgs/core/src/endOfISOWeekYear/index.tp.ts";

const date = new UTCDate("2024-12-30T00:00:00Z");
endOfISOWeekYear(date).toISOString();
// 2025-12-28T23:59:59.999Z
tpyEndOfISOWeekYear(date).toISOString();
// 2024-12-30T07:59:59.999Z
```

The same input fails with `UTCDateMini` and the `{ in: utc }` context. `startOfISOWeekYear` and `endOfDecade` also disagree with their existing implementations. A plain `Date` control still agrees.

`getDateTimeZone` recognizes the `timeZone` property used by `TZDate`, but neither UTC class exposes it, so the adapter falls back to `Temporal.Now.timeZoneId()`. An inherited `timeZone` getter returning `"UTC"` on `UTCDateMini` would also survive `toDate` cloning and cover both UTC classes.

This concerns the new Temporal source, which the current package build excludes; it is not a reproduction against the published Date-based implementation.
